---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Mike Schlottig and the systems-first approach behind Leverage AI."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div class="surface stack">
        <p class="eyebrow">Founder Context</p>
        <h1>Built in Grants Pass. Designed for Oregon service businesses.</h1>
        <p>
          Leverage AI exists because too many good service businesses get pushed into the wrong
          kind of help. They get generic agency retainers, disconnected freelancers, or flashy AI
          talk that never turns into a cleaner market position, a stronger website, or faster lead
          response.
        </p>
        <p>
          I built Leverage AI to connect the work that usually gets split between four vendors:
          getting found, earning trust, responding quickly, and measuring what turns into business.
          Your website should make the right first impression, your inquiry process should make it
          easy to take the next step, and the numbers should tell us whether the work is paying off.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="button primary" href="/contact/">Request the diagnostic audit</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="/pricing/">Review pricing</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside id="mike-schlottig" class="surface stack about-founder-card">
        <div class="about-headshot-frame">
          <img
            class="about-headshot"
            src="/images/founder/mike-schlottig.jpg"
            alt="Mike Schlottig outside a home in Oregon, founder of Leverage AI"
            loading="eager"
          />
        </div>
        <div class="stack about-founder-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Founder</p>
          <h2>Mike Schlottig</h2>
          <p>
            Founder of Leverage AI. Based in Southern Oregon. I work directly on your market
            research, website, search visibility, and lead-response process. There is no
            account-manager layer between the strategy we agree on and the work that gets built.
          </p>
          <p class="about-founder-note">
            The goal is simple: keep the person shaping the strategy close enough to the build that
            the work still has to survive real market conditions, real buyers, and real inquiry
            flow.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="meta-list about-meta-list">
          <li>Southern Oregon operator</li>
          <li>Brand surface first</li>
          <li>Workers-backed execution</li>
          <li>Direct strategy plus build</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell grid">
      <article class="surface stack">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why This Exists</p>
        <h2>Most local growth problems are system problems wearing a marketing label.</h2>
        <p>
          A business may think it has a traffic problem when the real issue is weak trust. It may
          think it needs a redesign when the bigger leak is slow response. It may think it needs
          more content when the public structure is too messy for buyers or search systems to
          trust.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is why the work here starts with diagnosis. The point is not to sell the biggest
          package. The point is to find the layer where the business is losing leverage first.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface stack">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why Oregon Matters</p>
        <h2>Local markets here do not behave like a national template.</h2>
        <p>
          Portland, Bend, Grants Pass, Roseburg, and Medford do not justify the same positioning,
          the same page architecture, or the same response strategy. Market density, buyer habits,
          competition, and trust signals shift from city to city.
        </p>
        <p>
          Operating from inside Oregon changes the work. The strategy can stay grounded in how
          people actually evaluate local service businesses here, not how a generic growth playbook
          says they should.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface stack">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why The Model Works</p>
        <h2>The solo-operator structure is a quality decision, not a constraint.</h2>
        <p>
          Every recommendation I make is close enough to execution that it has to survive contact
          with reality. There is no junior team translating strategy into something weaker. There
          is no account manager filtering the actual work. The person you talk to is the person
          doing the systems thinking and the build.
        </p>
        <p>
          That keeps the business lean, direct, and accountable. It also keeps the work closer to
          outcomes than to presentation theater.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div class="surface stack">
        <p class="eyebrow">What To Expect</p>
        <h2>Direct assessment, clear scope, and no fake certainty.</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The first move is to find where the current system is actually leaking.</li>
          <li>Recommendations should match the market and the business stage, not a canned package.</li>
          <li>Proof matters more than slogans, which is why reports and case studies are part of the public system.</li>
          <li>If the smartest answer is restraint or a smaller fix, that should be visible in the diagnosis.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="surface stack">
        <p class="eyebrow">Where To Go Next</p>
        <h2>See the work, understand the process, and decide whether the approach fits.</h2>
        <div class="stack">
          <a href="/knowledge-base/semantic-sync-for-local-search/">See how Google Business Profile and website signals should stay aligned.</a>
          <a href="/pricing/">See how the diagnostic audit is structured.</a>
          <a href="/portfolio/daley-organics/">Read the Daley Organics case study.</a>
          <a href="/reports/oregon-market-intel-infrastructure-report/">Read the Oregon market report.</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-founder-card {
    gap: 1.25rem;
    border-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 20%, var(--line));
    background:
      radial-gradient(circle at top right, var(--accent-glow), transparent 18rem),
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--surface-strong) 88%, black 12%);
  }

  .about-headshot-frame {
    padding: clamp(0.7rem, 1.5vw, 0.9rem);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 22%, var(--line));
    border-radius: 0.6rem;
    background:
      linear-gradient(180deg, color-mix(in oklab, var(--surface) 44%, black 56%), color-mix(in oklab, var(--surface-strong) 30%, black 70%));
  }

  .about-headshot {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 28%;
    border-radius: 0.45rem;
  }

  .about-founder-copy p {
    max-width: none;
  }

  .about-founder-note {
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--accent) 14%, var(--line));
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink) 82%, var(--muted));
  }

  .about-meta-list li {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
